If length &amp; breadth of a rectangle are (2x-1) &amp;
<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mo><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑥</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></math>
units respectively then find its area.<br />

questions.solution:
c) <math display=xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign="center" style="text-align: center"></mtd><mtd columnalign="center" style="text-align: center"><mo>⇒</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑎</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑏</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mo><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑎</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑏</mi><msup><mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>×</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑎</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑏</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑎</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑎</mi><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑏</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑏</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></mfrac></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign="center" style="text-align: center"></mtd><mtd columnalign="center" style="text-align: center"><mo>⇒</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mo><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑎</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑏</mi><mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mo><mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mo><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑎</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑏</mi><mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mo><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑎</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑏</mi><mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mo><mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mo><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑎</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑏</mi><mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>×</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mo><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑎</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑏</mi><mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑎</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑎</mi><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑏</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑏</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑎</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑎</mi><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑏</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑏</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></mfrac></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign="center" style="text-align: center"></mtd><mtd columnalign="center" style="text-align: center"><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑎</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑏</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{matrix}
 &amp; \mathit{\ } \Rightarrow \frac{\mathit{a}^{2} - \mathit{b}^{2}}{(\mathit{a} - \mathit{b})^{2}} \times \frac{\left( \mathit{a}^{3} - \mathit{b}^{3} \right)}{\left( \mathit{a}^{2} + \mathit{a}\mathit{b} + \mathit{b}^{2} \right)} \\
 &amp; \mathit{\ } \Rightarrow \frac{(\mathit{a} + \mathit{b})(\mathit{a} - \mathit{b})}{(\mathit{a} - \mathit{b})(\mathit{a} - \mathit{b})} \times \frac{(\mathit{a} - \mathit{b})\left( \mathit{a}^{2} + \mathit{a}\mathit{b} + \mathit{b}^{2} \right)}{\left( \mathit{a}^{2} + \mathit{a}\mathit{b} + \mathit{b}^{2} \right)} \\
 &amp; \mathit{\ } = \mathit{a} + \mathit{b}
\end{matrix}</annotation></math>

questions.text:
Write the following statement using symbol of variation.<br />
 The wave length of sound (
<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑙</mi></math>
) and its frequency (
<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑓</mi></math>
) are in inverse variation.<br />

here is an inverse variation between the wavelength (
<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑙</mi></math>
) of a sound and frequency (
<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑓</mi></math>
). How is this relation written using symbol of variation?<br />

questions.solution:
b) Suppose
<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><mi mathvariant="italic">▫</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>D</mi></mrow></math>
is a square &amp;
<math display=xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign="center" style="text-align: center"></mtd><mtd columnalign="center" style="text-align: center"><mi mathvariant="italic">𝐴</mi><mi mathvariant="italic">𝐵</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="italic">𝐵</mi><mi mathvariant="italic">𝐶</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><mrow><mtext mathvariant="normal"> </mtext></mrow><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign="center" style="text-align: center"></mtd><mtd columnalign="center" style="text-align: center"><mtext mathvariant="normal"> In </mtext><mo>△</mo><mi mathvariant="italic">𝐴</mi><mi mathvariant="italic">𝐵</mi><mi mathvariant="italic">𝐶</mi><mi>∠</mi><mi mathvariant="italic">𝐵</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>90</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{matrix}
 &amp; \mathit{A}\mathit{B} = \mathit{B}\mathit{C} = 10\text{ }cm \\
 &amp; \text{~In~} \bigtriangleup \mathit{A}\mathit{B}\mathit{C}\angle\mathit{B} = 90^{\circ}
\end{matrix}</annotation></math>
∴ Using phythagoras theorem
<math display=xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign="center" style="text-align: center"></mtd><mtd columnalign="center" style="text-align: center"><mi mathvariant="italic">𝐴</mi><msup><mi mathvariant="italic">𝐶</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="italic">𝐴</mi><msup><mi mathvariant="italic">𝐵</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="italic">𝐵</mi><msup><mi mathvariant="italic">𝐶</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign="center" style="text-align: center"></mtd><mtd columnalign="center" style="text-align: center"><mi mathvariant="italic">𝐴</mi><msup><mi mathvariant="italic">𝐶</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>100</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>200</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign="center" style="text-align: center"></mtd><mtd columnalign="center" style="text-align: center"><mi mathvariant="italic">𝐴</mi><mi mathvariant="italic">𝐶</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mn>200</mn></msqrt><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>100</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{matrix}
 &amp; \mathit{A}\mathit{C}^{2} = \mathit{A}\mathit{B}^{2} + \mathit{B}\mathit{C}^{2} \\
 &amp; \mathit{A}\mathit{C}^{2} = 10^{2} + 10^{2} = 100 + 100 = 200 \\
 &amp; \mathit{A}\mathit{C} = \sqrt{200} = \sqrt{100 \times 2} = 10\sqrt{2}
\end{matrix}</annotation></math>

b) Suppose the angles are
<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>x</mi><mi>&amp;</mi><mn>4</mn><mi>x</mi></mrow></math><br />
<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑥</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑥</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑥</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑥</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>360</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow></math>
(Sum of the angles of a quadrilateral is
<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><msup><mn>360</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></math>
)
<math display=xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign="center" style="text-align: center"></mtd><mtd columnalign="center" style="text-align: center"><mn>10</mn><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑥</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>360</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign="center" style="text-align: center"></mtd><mtd columnalign="center" style="text-align: center"><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑥</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>360</mn><mn>10</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>36</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{matrix}
 &amp; 10\mathit{x} = 360^{\circ} \\
 &amp; \mathit{x} = \frac{360}{10} = 36^{\circ}
\end{matrix}</annotation></math>
then the angles are
<math display=xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign="center" style="text-align: center"></mtd><mtd columnalign="center" style="text-align: center"><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑥</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>36</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑥</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>36</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>72</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign="center" style="text-align: center"></mtd><mtd columnalign="center" style="text-align: center"><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑥</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>36</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>108</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑥</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>36</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>144</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{matrix}
 &amp; \mathit{x} = 36^{\circ},2\mathit{x} = 2 \times 36^{\circ} = 72^{\circ} \\
 &amp; 3\mathit{x} = 3 \times 36^{\circ} = 108^{\circ},4\mathit{x} = 4 \times 36^{\circ} = 144^{\circ}
\end{matrix}</annotation></math>

questions.text:
If a polynomial (
<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑚</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><msup><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑚</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑚</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>
) is divided by (
<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑚</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>
), what is the quotient and remainder respectively?<br />

Average of marks of 15 boys is 40 &amp; average marks of 10 girls is
35 . Then find the average of marks of all students together (Boys &amp;
Girls)<br />

questions.solution:
d) <math display= xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign="center" style="text-align: center"></mtd><mtd columnalign="center" style="text-align: center"><mi>△</mi><mi mathvariant="italic">𝐴</mi><mi mathvariant="italic">𝐵</mi><mi mathvariant="italic">𝐶</mi><mo>≅</mo><mi>△</mi><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑃</mi><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑅</mi><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑄</mi><mo>⇒</mo><mi mathvariant="italic">𝐴</mi><mo>↔</mo><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑃</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="italic">𝐵</mi><mo>↔</mo><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑅</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="italic">𝐶</mi><mo>↔</mo><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑄</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign="center" style="text-align: center"><mo>∴</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign="center" style="text-align: center"><mi mathvariant="italic">𝐴</mi><mi mathvariant="italic">𝐵</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑃</mi><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑅</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="italic">𝐵</mi><mi mathvariant="italic">𝐶</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑅</mi><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑄</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="italic">𝐴</mi><mi mathvariant="italic">𝐶</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑃</mi><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑄</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign="center" style="text-align: center"></mtd><mtd columnalign="center" style="text-align: center"><mi>∠</mi><mi mathvariant="italic">𝐴</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>∠</mi><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑃</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>∠</mi><mi mathvariant="italic">𝐵</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>∠</mi><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑅</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>∠</mi><mi mathvariant="italic">𝐶</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>∠</mi><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑄</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{matrix}
 &amp; \bigtriangleup \mathit{A}\mathit{B}\mathit{C} \cong \bigtriangleup \mathit{P}\mathit{R}\mathit{Q} \Rightarrow \mathit{A} \leftrightarrow \mathit{P},\mathit{B} \leftrightarrow \mathit{R},\mathit{C} \leftrightarrow \mathit{Q} \\
\therefore &amp; \mathit{A}\mathit{B} = \mathit{P}\mathit{R},\mathit{B}\mathit{C} = \mathit{R}\mathit{Q},\mathit{A}\mathit{C} = \mathit{P}\mathit{Q} \\
 &amp; \angle\mathit{A} = \angle\mathit{P},\angle\mathit{B} = \angle\mathit{R},\angle\mathit{C} = \angle\mathit{Q}
\end{matrix}</annotation></math>

c)  Volume of cylinder
<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="italic">𝜋</mi><msup><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑟</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi mathvariant="italic">ℎ</mi></mrow></math>
<math display=xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign="center" style="text-align: center"></mtd><mtd columnalign="center" style="text-align: center"><mo>∴</mo><mn>1078</mn><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>22</mn><mn>7</mn></mfrac><mo>×</mo><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑟</mi><mo>×</mo><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑟</mi><mo>×</mo><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑟</mi><mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mo><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑟</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="italic">ℎ</mi><mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign="center" style="text-align: center"></mtd><mtd columnalign="center" style="text-align: center"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1078</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><mn>22</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑟</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign="center" style="text-align: center"></mtd><mtd columnalign="center" style="text-align: center"><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign="center" style="text-align: center"><mn>49</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>=</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑟</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mtd><mtd columnalign="center" style="text-align: center"><mo>∴</mo><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑟</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign="center" style="text-align: center"><mo>∴</mo><mi mathvariant="italic">𝑟</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="italic">ℎ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></mtd><mtd columnalign="center" style="text-align: center"></mtd></mtr></mtable></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{matrix}
 &amp; \mathit{\ }\therefore 1078 = \frac{22}{7} \times \mathit{r} \times \mathit{r} \times \mathit{r}(\mathit{r} = \mathit{h}) \\
 &amp; \frac{1078 \times 7}{22} = \mathit{r}^{3} \\
 &amp; \begin{matrix}
49 \times 7 = \mathit{r}^{3} &amp; \therefore\mathit{r} = 7 \\
\therefore\ \mathit{r} = \mathit{h} = 7 &amp; 
\end{matrix}
\end{matrix}</annotation></math>
